==Controversy==
Before the series started, there was speculation Paul Hollywood would not appear in the show after his marriage broke down.<ref>{{cite news |title=Next on menu after marriage break-up: Paul Hollywood puts his name on pants |url= http://www.thetimes.co.uk/tto/life/celebrity/article3870049.ece |archive-url= https://archive.today/20200226142405/http://www.thetimes.co.uk/tto/life/celebrity/article3870049.ece |url-status= dead |archive-date= 26 February 2020 |author=Alex Ralph |date=16 September 2013 |work=[[The Times]] }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.nydailynews.com/entertainment/tv-movies/american-baking-hot-rumor-on-set-romance-article-1.1356708 |title=Reported affair between judges Paul Hollywood and Marcela Valladolid adds spice to baking show |author=Don Kaplan |work=New York Daily News |date=29 May 2013 }}</ref> Mary Berry, however, was claimed to have supported Hollywood over rumours of his possible sacking by BBC; and the BBC denied the rumours and said that his job was safe.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/celebritynews/10082294/Paul-Hollywoods-job-on-The-Great-British-Bake-Off-is-safe-BBC-says.html |title=Paul Hollywood's job on The Great British Bake Off is safe, BBC says  |author=Sam Marsden |date=27 May 2013 |work=The Daily Telegraph }}</ref>

During the series, there were accusations of Paul Hollywood's favouritism towards Ruby Tandoh,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.whatsontv.co.uk/tv-news/news/paul-hollywood-shocks-bake-off-fans-by-setting-them-straight-on-train |title=Paul Hollywood shocks Bake Off fans by setting them straight on train |work=What's on TV |date=9 October 2013 |access-date=11 October 2013 |archive-date=2 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402194441/http://www.whatsontv.co.uk/tv-news/news/paul-hollywood-shocks-bake-off-fans-by-setting-them-straight-on-train |url-status=dead }}</ref> and personal attacks on Tandoh by various people including the chef [[Raymond Blanc]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/tv/news/raymond-blanc-rows-with-great-british-bake-offs-ruby-tandoh-on-twitter-8894652.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220618/https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/tv/news/raymond-blanc-rows-with-great-british-bake-offs-ruby-tandoh-on-twitter-8894652.html |archive-date=18 June 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Raymond Blanc rows with Great British Bake Off's Ruby Tandoh on Twitter |author=Liam O'Brien |date= 21 October 2013 |work=The Independent }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=The Great British Bake Off: why did our show attract so much vitriol? |url= https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2013/oct/22/great-british-bake-off-ruby-dandoh |author=Ruby Tandoh |work=
The Guardian |date= 22 October 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2013/oct/21/great-british-bake-off-ruby-tandoh-hostility |title=The Great British Bake-Off: Ruby Tandoh doesn't deserve this hostility |author=Sarah Ditum |work = The Guardian |date= 21 October 2013 }}</ref> Both Paul Hollywood and Ruby Tandoh denied the accusation.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/tv/news/paul-hollywood-denies-favouring-great-british-bake-offs-ruby-tandoh-i-think-kimberleys-far-prettier-8881047.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220618/https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/tv/news/paul-hollywood-denies-favouring-great-british-bake-offs-ruby-tandoh-i-think-kimberleys-far-prettier-8881047.html |archive-date=18 June 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Paul Hollywood denies favouring Great British Bake Off's Ruby Tandoh: 'I think Kimberley's far prettier' |author=Daisy Wyatt |date= 15 October 2013 |work=The Independent }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/tv/tv-news/great-british-bake-off-ruby-2369469 |title=Great British Bake Off beauty Ruby Tandoh definitely does NOT fancy silver fox Paul Hollywood |work= Daily Mirror | author=Carl Greenwood  |date= 14 October 2013 }}</ref>

==Post-show careers==
[[Frances Quinn]] wrote a book, ''Quinntessential Baking'', released on 17 August 2015.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.dailypost.co.uk/whats-on/food-drink-news/former-great-british-bake-winner-9886613 |title=Former Great British Bake Off winner Frances Quinn shares recipes for her yummy treats from new book|date= 29 August 2015|author=Georgia Morgan }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/foodanddrink/10729680/Frances-Quinn-Bake-Off-was-an-endurance-test.html |title=Frances Quinn: 'Bake Off was an endurance test' |author= Leah Hyslop |date=1 Apr 2014 |work=Daily Telegraph }}</ref>

[[Ruby Tandoh]] has written three books, ''Crumb: The Baking Book'', published on 25 September 2014, ''Flavour: Eat What You Love'' in 2016<ref name="nyt-eat-what-you-love">{{cite news|last1=Rao|first1=Tejal|title=Ruby Tandoh Just Wants You to Eat What You Love|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/01/03/dining/ruby-tandoh-flavour-eat-what-you-love.html|accessdate=5 January 2017|work=The New York Times|date=3 January 2017}}</ref> and ''Eat Up: Food, Appetite and Eating What You Want'' in 2018.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2018/feb/05/ruby-tandoh-fine-to-enjoy-ready-meal-eat-up-interview |title=Ruby Tandoh: 'I want to remind people that it's fine to enjoy a ready meal'|first=Killian |last=Fox|work=The Guardian |date=5 February 2020 }}</ref>  She took half a year out from the university after the show, but has returned to complete her philosophy and history of art course at [[University College London]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/foodanddrink/foodanddrinknews/11113772/Ruby-Tandoh-on-GBBO-Twitter-trolls-and-her-new-baking-book.html |title=Ruby Tandoh on GBBO, Twitter trolls and her new baking book |author=Siân Ranscombe |date= 26 September 2014 |work=The Daily Telegraph }}</ref> She wrote a column on baking for ''[[The Guardian]]''.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/profile/ruby-tandoh |title=Ruby Tandoh |work=The Guardian }}</ref>

[[Beca Lyne-Pirkis]] presented a Welsh language cookery show ''Becws'' for [[S4C]] starting 11 September 2014.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.walesonline.co.uk/whats-on/food-drink-news/im-happy-being-only-welshie-7535994 |title='I'm happy about being the only Welshie on the Great British Bake Off,' says Beca Lyne-Pirkis |date=30 July 2014 |author= Kate Rees |work=Wales Online }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.southwales-eveningpost.co.uk/Bake-s-Beca-new-baking-Becws/story-22863172-detail/story.html |title=Bake Off's Beca is back with new baking show Becws |author=Ruth Dawson |work= South Wales Evening Post  |date=2 September 2014 }}</ref> She later participated in the 2017 rendition of ''The Great Christmas Bake Off''.

Glenn Cosby left teaching and launched his own touring stage show ''Bake It Big'' in 2014.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.foodheavenmag.com/bake-it-big-with-glenn-cosby-on-tour-this-summer/ |title=Bake it Big with Glenn Cosby – on tour this summer! |date=8 May 2014 |work=Food Heaven }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.exeterexpressandecho.co.uk/Great-British-Bake-star-Glenn-Cosby-returns-Bake/story-21226670-detail/story.html |title=Great British Bake Off star Glenn Cosby returns to Bake It Big |work=Exeter Express and Echo|date= 12 June 2014}}</ref> That same year, Cosby release his book ''Tasty Travels''.

Ali Imdad has set up a bakery business, and become a full-time baker.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.birminghammail.co.uk/whats-on/great-british-bake-off-contestants-7545213 |title=Whatever happened to the Great British Bake Off bakers from the Midlands? |date=2 August 2014 |author= Roz Laws |work=Birmingham Mail }}</ref> He later participated in the 2016 rendition of ''The Great Christmas Bake Off''.

Howard Middleton participated in the 2016 rendition of ''The Great New Year's Bake Off''.

==Ratings==
The show achieved some of the highest ratings seen on BBC Two. According to overnight data, the final episode was seen by 9.1 million viewers at its peak, and averaging over 8 million, more than twice the number of viewers on BBC One and ITV.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2013/oct/23/great-british-bake-off-final-frances-quinn |title=Great British Bake Off final: 9m watch Frances Quinn win |author=John Plunkett| work=The Guardian |date= 23 October 2013 }}</ref>  This episode is the highest-rated show ever on BBC2 under the ratings system first introduced in 2002, beating the previous record set by an episode of ''[[Top Gear (2002 TV series)|Top Gear]]'' in 2007.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/bbcs-great-british-bake-ends-650312 |title=BBC's 'Great British Bake Off' Ends Season 4 With Record Ratings |author=Georg Szalai |work=The Hollywood Reporter }}</ref> The highest rating for BBC2 of all-time is The [[1985 World Snooker Championship|1985 Snooker World Championship]] Final which peaked with 18.5m viewers. Series 4 had an average of viewing figure of 7.4 million.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.broadcastnow.co.uk/ratings/bbc1-rises-to-the-challenge/5082034.article |title=BBC1 rises to the challenge|date=22 January 2015 |author= Stephen Price |work=Broadcast }}</ref> During the length of the series, the only programmes to have beaten the show on a Tuesday night were [[EastEnders]] and [[Emmerdale]].

Official episode viewing figures are from [[BARB]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.barb.co.uk/whats-new/weekly-top-30|title=Weekly Top 30 Programmes|publisher=[[Broadcasters' Audience Research Board]]|accessdate=16 September 2015}}</ref>
